the best programmers are up to 28 times  better than the worst programmers quote  from robert glass american software  engineer and writer  great programmers are very rare in fact  only one percent of developers in the  world don't just write solid code but  have important intangible traits that  set them apart from the crowd  today i will share with you seven  intangible traits of great developers  and would like you to comment below  which one is the most important  so  let's start with the first one positive  attitude having a positive attitude  means being optimistic about situations  interactions and yourself  people with positive attitude remain  hopeful and see the best even in  difficult situations  in contrast those with negative attitude  may be more pessimistic and disagreeable  and typically expect the worst outcome  in tough situations  let's say a product needs to be released  to the market a great programmer will  step up and get the product released  whenever possible because they care and  their positive attitude helps them focus  on solutions and meet all requirements  developers with positive attitude are  willing to go the distance to get the  job done and bring their best every day  they don't let their ego get in the way  of constructive criticism they care  about product's success  a positive attitude is one of the key  qualities of a great software developer  second trait of the great developers is  strong communication skills  communication occurs in many different  ways and contexts from writing and  speaking to body language we use various  skills to convey and obtain information  however demonstrating strong  communication skills is about being able  to convey information to others in a  simple and unambiguous way  great programmers with strong  communication skills are able to  listen carefully  ask the right questions  understand problems and concepts quickly  break them down into hypotheses  and propose solutions in a coherent  manner  strong communication skills directly  correlate with strong programming skills  next trait of great developers  is effective time and task management  time management is the strategy of  planning out your available time and  controlling the amount of time you spend  on specific tasks in order to work more  efficiently  when you effectively manage your time  and tasks you will improve your  performance produce better work deliver  work on time  reduce your stress boost your confidence  become more efficient  take on new opportunities and grow in a  sustainable manner  effective time management comes easier  some people than to others but everyone  can develop habits to become more  effective  those who are considered as great  developers have developed excellent time  and task management skills  next trait of great developers  is quick learning ability  the best skill anyone can have working  in a rapidly changing industry is quick  learning ability  a quick learner is someone who is  capable of understanding new information  at rapid pace  it also goes beyond just comprehension  you need to be able to show that you can  apply what you learn to your work  great programmers are usually amazing  self-learners  they know how to stay updated and  relevant they have the ability to teach  themselves new languages and  technologies quickly  and often do so out of personal interest  outside of work they have the ability to  process information and make connections  on the fly every programmer will  experience a situation where they don't  know the answer but great programmers  will find different resources  talk to the right people and find a  solution no matter what  next trait is deep and broad technical  experience  every software engineer has a skill set  made up of different skills they've  acquired a skill set is deep and narrow  if you've mastered one to two skills and  little else and it's broad and shallow  if you can do a little bit of everything  without being an expert in any field  great developers have mastered both deep  and broad skills  working with different technologies long  enough they became experts and are  competent with many other technologies  therefore great developers with deep and  broad technical experience are able to  arrive at optimal solutions quickly by  combining their cognitive abilities and  diverse industry experience  they follow coding standards and write  code that is understandable and  commented so that it can easily be  passed on to someone else  an experienced developer is well versed  in best practices like agile development  and task management software they also  have mastered version control different  development environments and the process  of deploying applications  on the projects that require unrelated  skill set combined in unpredictable ways  broadly skilled engineer with a deep  skill set will contribute usefully to  most of them and will match precise  requirements  a great team player is another quality  of premium developers  sports teams are perfect examples of how  many players working together can  achieve much more than one player who is  acting alone  teams are created for several reasons  they may need to deliver a one-time  project or work together on an ongoing  basis either way teamworking is such a  vital way of completing projects  successful teams require people with  different strengths to work together in  a new effective way and at greater speed  to achieve top-notch results  a great team player is someone who  understands his role in the team uses  his strengths demonstrates reliability  communicates constructively  listens actively  embraces collaboration committed to his  team flexible and treats others in a  respectful and supportive manner  great team players always work on  developing and refining the skills that  will help them to make a valuable  contribution to the team they are in  and the seventh trait of great  developers is big picture focus big  picture focus requires big picture  thinking the ability to grasp abstract  concepts ideas and possibilities  imagine driving on the london bridge you  look at the left and the right only  while driving  well hopefully not you trained yourself  to cover both your left and right sides  as well as look back and far ahead in  front of you  if you only focused on a tiny word on  the bumper sticker of the vehicle  directly in front of you  you risk getting into a pretty bad  accident  that's essentially what big picture  thinking is looking at the entirety of a  plan or situation  in contrast a detailed oriented person  delves into the specifics of a project  or process in doing so they may lose  sight of the plan or the big picture  but this doesn't mean that one thinking  modality is right and another is wrong  instead they are both necessary to  function effectively and great  programmers master them both  in truth there's no way to say for sure  how well you might do as a programmer  until you try it still it's not a  coincidence that the best programmers  all share the traits above  with them it's just easier to succeed in  a challenging field like programming and  without them you'd be likely to struggle  it's important to understand that coding  is the initial door to success for  programmers but the desire to learn  should extend well beyond programming  knowledge because there is much more to  what makes a great programmer if you  found this video useful please like and  share it also make sure to subscribe to  the coding tech channel and click on the  notification bell  have a wonderful day
 

